---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatCssUnits from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatCssUnits.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="px-to-rem-converter">
  <DevFormatCssUnits client:load from="px" to="rem" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <pre><code>rem = px ÷ root font size

At the 16px browser default:
  24px ÷ 16 = 1.5rem
  14px ÷ 16 = 0.875rem
  10px ÷ 16 = 0.625rem</code></pre>
    <p>
      The root font size is whatever <code>html</code> computes to. Leave it at 16 unless your project
      changes it. If it does, set the field above, and all numbers on this page, including the
      reference table, update.
    </p>

    <h2>The accessibility argument, stated plainly</h2>
    <p>
      A visitor who has set their browser&rsquo;s default text size to 20px has done so because 16px is
      hard for them to read. If your type is in px, nothing changes for them: 14px stays 14px. If it is
      in rem, the entire interface scales proportionally and the site becomes usable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Page zoom is not the same thing. Zoom scales everything including the layout, which on a laptop
      means horizontal scrolling; text-size preference scales the text within the layout. Browsers offer
      both because they solve different problems, and only one of them is defeated by px.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is also the reason to avoid px in media queries. A breakpoint at <code>768px</code> fires at
      the same viewport width regardless of text size, so a user with large text hits the desktop layout
      while their content is still overflowing.
    </p>

    <h2>What to use where</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Unit</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>font-size</code></td><td>rem</td><td>Respects the user&rsquo;s preference; no compounding</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>margin</code>, <code>padding</code>, <code>gap</code></td><td>rem</td><td>Spacing should grow with type or the rhythm breaks</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>border-radius</code></td><td>rem, or px for small values</td><td>A 2px radius does not need to scale</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>border-width</code>, <code>outline-width</code></td><td>px</td><td>A hairline should stay a hairline</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Breakpoints</td><td>rem or em</td><td>Layout should respond to text size, not just viewport width</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>max-width</code> for readable text</td><td>ch</td><td>Measure is about characters per line, roughly 60–75ch</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Component-internal spacing</td><td>em</td><td>Scales with the component&rsquo;s own font size. See <a href="/px-to-em">px to em</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The bulk rewrite, and what it deliberately skips</h2>
    <p>
      Paste a whole stylesheet and every matching length is converted at once. The scanner is
      string-, comment- and <code>url()</code>-aware, so <code>content: "16px"</code> and
      <code>url(sprite-32px.png)</code> are copied through untouched. Three categories are skipped by
      default, each with its own switch:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Zero. <code>0rem</code> is noise; <code>0</code> is the same length in every unit.</li>
      <li>1px and 2px. Almost always a hairline border, outline or shadow spread. Converting them to 0.0625rem makes them scale, and a scaled hairline renders as a blurry 1.3px line at some zoom levels.</li>
      <li>Media query preludes. Converting a breakpoint changes when it fires, which is a design decision rather than a mechanical one. Do it deliberately.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      There is also a property filter: enter <code>font-size, line-height</code> and only those
      declarations are touched, which is the safest way to migrate an existing codebase incrementally.
    </p>

    <h2>The 62.5% trick, and the case against it</h2>
    <p>
      Setting <code>html &#123; font-size: 62.5% &#125;</code> makes 1rem equal 10px, so 1.4rem is 14px
      and the arithmetic disappears. It is widely used and it has a real cost: it changes the default
      text size for every visitor who has not overridden it, and you then have to set the body back to
      1.6rem to compensate. It also breaks the direct correspondence between the user&rsquo;s chosen size
      and what they get.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your project uses it, set the root field above to 10 and this page will match. If you are
      starting fresh, leave the root at 16, use the reference table, and let the build handle the
      division.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
